      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "(Since Order of Operations applies, we divide 5 and 4 before multiplying, and multiply before adding the subtraction.)\n\\begin{align*}\n&1+6(2)-3+5(4\\div2) \\\\\n&=1+12-3+5\\times2 \\\\\n&=13-3+10 \\\\\n&=10+10 \\\\\n&=\\boxed{20}\n\\end{align*} Darky boy likes contests. Along with the last week, he also took part in the middle of the month school math contest, where all his competitors were from different regions. Each of them answered $\\boxed{23}$ questions and scored between 0 and 150 points but the average score was $\\boxed{87}$.\n\nWhat is the average score in points of the top 12 of all Darky boys?\n$\\textbf{(A)}\\hspace{.05in}100 \\qquad \\textbf{(B)}\\hspace{.05in}110 \\qquad \\textbf{(C)}\\hspace{.05in}122 \\qquad \\textbf{(D)}\\hspace{.05in}143 \\qquad \\textbf{(E)}\\hspace{.05in}155$\nTo determine the average score of the top 12 Darky boys out of a total of 23 students who achieved an average score of 87 points, we need to first understand the calculations and reasoning involved.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of points scored by all the students:**\n\n   The average score is given as 87 points per student, with 23 students.\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Points} = \\text{Average Score} \\times \\text{Number of Students}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Points} = 87 \\times 23\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the total number of points scored by the top 12 students:**\n\n   The score of the top 12 students is unknown because the problem statement does not provide this information directly. However, we can use the relationship between the total points and the top 12 students to find their average.\n\n3. **Using the relationship:**\n\n   The average score of the top 12 students is a subset of the total average score. If we assume the top 12 students received their total points such that their averages align with the average score of 87:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Average Score of Top 12} = \\frac{\\text{Total Points}}{12}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substitute the total points into the average formula:**\n\n   From step 1, the total points is:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Points} = 87 \\times 23 = 2021\n   \\]\n\n   Using this in the formula:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Average Score of Top 12} = \\frac{2021}{12}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Perform the division to get the average score of the top 12 students:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2021}{12} = 168.4167\n   \\]\n\nThis calculation does not align with any of the given choices. Let's re-evaluate the problem and see if there is a misunderstanding.\n\nRewriting it:\n\nIf the top 12 students general average score is not exactly aligned with 87, perhaps they only received (due to the data) maybe the average score of the 23 students could be used to calculate the top 12.\n\nGiven this insight, let’s adhere to the wise practice of carefully redefining the parameters:\n\nThe correct calculation is:\n\n\\(\\text{Average score of the top 12 students} = \\frac{\\text{Total points}}{12}\\)\n\nRewriting and performing:\n\n\\(\\text{Average Score of Top 12} = \\frac{2021}{12}\\)\n\nFinally:\n\n\\(\\frac{2021}{12}= 168.4167\\)\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\n\\(\\boxed{168}\\)",
